  "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+5)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e89{t}^{2}-8t,\\,6{t}^{2}-2t\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(9{t}^{2}-8t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}-2t)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e818t-8,\\,12t-2\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =\u27e818(1)-8,\\,12(1)-2\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e818-8,\\,12-2\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e810,\\,10\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(1)|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|\u27e810,\\,10\u27e9| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{10}^{2}+{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100+100} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{200} \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the acceleration at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}$ is $10\\sqrt{2}{\\text{\u00a0m/s}}^{2}.$",
